12-TERRAFORM INIT?

terraform init is the first command you run in a new or existing Terraform configuration.
It initializes the working directory and prepares Terraform to create or manage infrastructure.

Think of it as setting up Terraform to “understand” your project.

2️⃣ What Happens When You Run terraform init

Initialize Backend

If you defined a backend block, Terraform will configure remote state storage.

Example: S3, Terraform Cloud, Azure Storage.

If backend requires locking, it will prepare that too.

Download Providers

Terraform reads your required_providers in terraform {} block.

Downloads the correct provider plugins to .terraform directory.

Initialize Modules

If you are using module blocks (local or remote), Terraform downloads and initializes them.

Prepare Working Directory

Creates hidden .terraform directory with:

Provider plugins

Module cache

Backend configuration

Key Points:

Always run terraform init when:

Starting a new project

Pulling an existing Terraform config from version control

Adding a new provider or module

Only needs to be run once per working directory, unless you change backend/providers.

Creates .terraform directory (contains providers, modules, and backend info).

terraform init is the setup step in Terraform that prepares the directory by configuring backend, downloading providers, and initializing modules.
Without it, you cannot run plan or apply.





