Health check probes in Kubernetes are used to monitor the health and readiness of containers inside Pods. There are three main types of probes:
🩺 1. Liveness Probe
Purpose: Checks if the container is alive or still running properly.
If fails: Kubernetes restarts the container.
🧠 Real-time example:
If your app gets stuck in a deadlock, the liveness probe fails and K8s restarts it.
⏳ 2. Readiness Probe
Purpose: Checks if the container is ready to serve traffic.
If fails: Pod is removed from Service load balancing (but not restarted).
🧠 Real-time example:
App might take time to load data on startup. Until it’s ready, K8s won’t send requests to it.
💤 3. Startup Probe
Purpose: Checks if the app has started successfully (used for slow-starting apps).
If fails: Kubernetes restarts the container.
Disables other probes until it passes.
🧠 Real-time example:
If your app takes 2 minutes to boot, startup probe prevents liveness from killing it too early.
⚙️ Probe types:
httpGet: Check a URL endpoint.
tcpSocket: Check if a port is open.
exec: Run a command inside the container.

