addons update:
| Addon          | Job                            |
| -------------- | ------------------------------ |
| **VPC-CNI**    | Gives IPs to pods (networking) |
| **CoreDNS**    | Resolves service names (DNS)   |
| **kube-proxy** | Routes service traffic to pods |
steps:
eksctl get addon --cluster <cluster-name>
Upgrade addons (recommended method)
Using eksctl (easiest)
Upgrade VPC-CNI
eksctl upgrade addon \
  --name vpc-cni \
  --cluster <cluster-name> \
  --approve

Upgrade CoreDNS
eksctl upgrade addon \
  --name coredns \
  --cluster <cluster-name> \
  --approve

Upgrade kube-proxy
eksctl upgrade addon \
  --name kube-proxy \
  --cluster <cluster-name> \
  --approve

Verify the upgrade
kubectl get pods -n kube-system

Check:

all aws-node pods running (VPC-CNI)

coredns pods running

kube-proxy DaemonSet updated

Get DaemonSet version:

kubectl get ds kube-proxy -n kube-system -o wide

Check version inside image:
kubectl describe deployment coredns -n kube-system | grep Image
kubectl describe ds kube-proxy -n kube-system | grep Image

ORDER OF UPGRADE (VERY IMPORTANT)

VPC-CNI → first

kube-proxy → second

CoreDNS → last

This avoids networking issues.

⚠️ COMMON ISSUE

If addons are managed by Helm or custom-manifests → eksctl upgrade will fail.

You must remove the old unmanaged add-on, then install EKS-managed one.





