26-What is toYaml in Helm?

toYaml is a Helm template function that converts a Helm object or map into properly formatted YAML text.

It’s used when you want to take structured data (from values.yaml) and print it neatly into your Kubernetes manifests.

🧠 Why Use It?

Sometimes you have nested values in your values.yaml, like:

env:
  - name: APP_MODE
    value: production
  - name: LOG_LEVEL
    value: debug


Instead of writing long loops in your template, you can just:

env:
{{ toYaml .Values.env | indent 2 }}


This automatically converts the YAML structure into correctly indented YAML under the env: key.
Example 2 — With toYaml

values.yaml:

env:
  - name: APP_MODE
    value: production
  - name: LOG_LEVEL
    value: debug


deployment.yaml:

containers:
  - name: myapp
    image: nginx
    env:
{{ toYaml .Values.env | indent 6 }}


When rendered:

containers:
  - name: myapp
    image: nginx
    env:
      - name: APP_MODE
        value: production
      - name: LOG_LEVEL
        value: debug


✅ Proper indentation
✅ Clean and reusable
toYaml alone doesn’t indent lines.
You must use | indent N to align it under the correct YAML key.

Example:

annotations:
{{ toYaml .Values.podAnnotations | indent 4 }}




What is a with block in Kubernetes (Helm)?

In Helm templates, a with block is used to set the context to a specific part of a values file, so you don’t have to keep writing long value paths.

🧠 Syntax:
{{- with .Values.myapp }}
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: {{ .name }}
data:
  key: {{ .key }}
{{- end }}

🔍 Explanation:

.Values.myapp → refers to a section in the values.yaml file.

Inside the with block, the dot (.) now points to .Values.myapp.

This means you can just write .name instead of .Values.myapp.name.

📘 Example:

values.yaml

myapp:
  name: my-config
  key: "value123"


configmap.yaml

{{- with .Values.myapp }}
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: {{ .name }}
data:
  key: {{ .key }}
{{- end }}


Rendered output:

apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: my-config
data:
  key: value123

✅ Benefits:

Makes templates cleaner and shorter.

Avoids repeating long paths like .Values.myapp.name.

Helps group related values logically.




